WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 233: Portugal: The Great Lisbon Earthquake of 1755, the Marques de Pombal, and the City Reborn

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.000
[Rebecca] Welcome back to most fascinating stories.

2
00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.120
[Rebecca] Today, we witness one of the most terrifying natural disasters in human history.

3
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:28.720
[Rebecca] The catastrophic earthquake, tsunami, and fire storm that obliterated the glittering imperial city of Lisbon on All Saints' Day in 1755, and the visionary statesman who rebuilt the capital from the ashes, the Marx de Pombo.

4
00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:42.320
[George] On the morning of Saturday, November 1, 1755, Lisbon was one of the wealthiest, most dazzling cities in the world, the capital of a global empire enriched by the diamond and gold mines of Brazil.

5
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:48.160
[George] It was All Saints' Day, one of the most sacred holidays in the Catholic liturgical calendar.

6
00:00:48.160 --> 00:00:56.560
[George] The city's 250,000 citizens had gathered inside Lisbon's 60 churches, convents, and the grand Gothic cathedral.

7
00:00:56.560 --> 00:01:10.000
[George] Tens of thousands of tallow and wax candles blazed on church altars, illuminating intricate gilded woodwork, marble statues and silk tapestries, while solemn choral music echoed into the sunny morning air.

8
00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:15.840
[George] The bells of dozens of bell towers told across the seven hills overlooking the Tagus River.

9
00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:29.200
[George] Lisbon felt holy, prosperous and secure, unaware that beneath the ocean floor 200 miles southwest in the Atlantic Fault Lines, monumental tectonic forces were about to unleash apocalyptic destruction.

10
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:42.720
[George] On that bright All Saints morning, the waters of the Tagus were packed with hundreds of merchant galleons, caravels and fishing boats unloading luxury spices, gold dust from Minas Geras, and barrels of port wine.

11
00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:56.400
[George] Wealthy Portuguese aristocrats lived in opulent marble townhouses along the Chayado, their private salons decorated with Chinese porcelain, Brazilian gold filigree, and priceless oil paintings imported from Italy.

12
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:08.040
[George] The wealthy Lisbon elite lived lives of secluded luxury, traveling between their palatial estates and church services in gilded sedan chairs carried by African footmen in vibrant livery.

13
00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:15.480
[George] At 9, 40 in the morning, a deep, subterranean rumble like heavy artillery shook the foundations of the city.

14
00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:19.480
[George] Dogs barked furiously, birds fled into the sky.

15
00:02:19.480 --> 00:02:29.800
[George] Seconds later, a cataclysmic earthquake measuring an estimated 8.5 to 9 on the modern Richter scale struck Lisbon with terrifying, unrelenting violence.

16
00:02:29.800 --> 00:02:34.760
[George] For six agonizing minutes, the earth heaved like storm-tossed ocean waves.

17
00:02:34.760 --> 00:02:44.760
[George] Stone cathedral vaults cracked open, massive bell towers toppled into crowded streets, five-story limestone palaces collapsed like houses of cards.

18
00:02:44.760 --> 00:02:49.880
[George] Inside packed churches, roofs crushed thousands of worshippers kneeling at communion.

19
00:02:49.880 --> 00:02:57.160
[George] Deep fissures, some sixteen feet wide, split open the cobblestone streets, swallowing people and carriages.

20
00:02:57.160 --> 00:03:13.400
[George] Thick clouds of limestone dust and pulverized masonry rose thousands of feet into the sky, blotting out the bright morning sun and plunging the city into suffocating, pitch-black darkness, illuminated only by the terrified screams of dying and trapped citizens.

21
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:27.320
[George] Seismologists estimate that the shockwaves from the Lisbon earthquake traveled across Europe at four miles per second, rattling church chandeliers in Switzerland and causing water in Scottish locks to slosh violently in their basins.

22
00:03:27.320 --> 00:03:41.080
[George] The primary seismic rupture occurred along the Azor's Gibraltar Transform Fault, unleashing energy equivalent to forty million tons of TNT and triggering geological tremors felt as far away as Finland and North Africa.

23
00:03:41.080 --> 00:03:52.960
[George] The geological fault line ruptured with such violence that church bells in Madrid, 400 miles away, began tolling spontaneously as the ground trembled beneath the Spanish capital.

24
00:03:52.960 --> 00:04:03.040
[George] Thousands of dazed, bleeding survivors fled the collapsing hills toward the wide, open waterfront of the Tagas River, seeking safety away from falling rubble.

25
00:04:03.040 --> 00:04:09.760
[George] When they arrived at the grand marble quay of Caes de Pedra, they witnessed a bizarre and terrifying spectacle.

26
00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:11.520
[George] The river had vanished.

27
00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:21.760
[George] The water had retreated far out into the Atlantic, leaving the riverbed completely exposed, littered with stranded shipwrecks, lost cargo, and flapping fish.

28
00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:33.920
[George] Suddenly, lookouts screamed in horror as a monstrous wall of dark water, forty feet high, appeared on the horizon, roaring up the Tagas estuary from the Atlantic at 50 miles per hour.

29
00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:47.200
[George] The tsunami crashed onto the waterfront with colossal kinetic force, engulfing the Marble Quay, sweeping hundreds of ships onto the streets, and drowning thousands of fleeing refugees in swirling, debris-filled waters.

30
00:04:47.200 --> 00:04:53.840
[George] Two subsequent tsunami waves struck in rapid succession, washing entire neighborhoods out to sea.

31
00:04:53.840 --> 00:05:05.760
[George] The tsunami waves crossed the entire Atlantic Ocean in under ten hours, smashing docks in Martinique and Barbados and causing water levels to rise by several feet in Caribbean harbors.

32
00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:18.160
[George] Eyewitnesses along the Tagus described the incoming tsunami wave as a colossal black mountain of foam that roared with the sound of a thousand thunders, destroying over three thousand wooden vessels in the harbor.

33
00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:30.440
[George] The sudden withdrawal of the Tagus River exposed the ancient mud and discarded anchors of five centuries of maritime navigation, creating a deceptive silence before the oceanic wall of water struck.

34
00:05:30.440 --> 00:05:35.320
[George] As the floodwaters receded, a third nightmare consumed the ruined city.

35
00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:35.880
[George] Fire.

36
00:05:36.120 --> 00:05:46.600
[George] The thousands of candles that had been burning on church altars, combined with cooking fires in collapsed kitchens and hearths, ignited wooden beams, furniture and tapestries.

37
00:05:46.600 --> 00:05:57.800
[George] Driven by a fierce northeasterly wind, individual blazes coalesced into a gigantic, self-sustaining firestorm that raged unchecked across Lisbon for six days and nights.

38
00:05:57.800 --> 00:06:03.320
[George] The heat was so intense that bronze church bells melted into glowing liquid in their belfries.

39
00:06:03.320 --> 00:06:16.440
[George] The fire incinerated irreplaceable cultural and historic treasures, the Royal Palace of Ribera, containing 70,000 volumes in the Royal Library and paintings by Titian, Rubens and Correggio.

40
00:06:16.440 --> 00:06:27.160
[George] The National Archives containing the original logs and hand-drawn navigational charts of Vasco da Gama and Bartolo Mudayas, and hundreds of priceless historic convents.

41
00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:36.040
[George] Between 30,000 and 60,000 citizens perished in the triple catastrophe, leaving Lisbon a smoking, blackened wasteland of rubble.

42
00:06:36.040 --> 00:06:49.400
[George] The Lisbon firestorm burned so ferociously that it created its own hurricane force convective winds, sucking oxygen from collapsed basements and suffocating hundreds of survivors who had sought shelter in stone cellars.

43
00:06:49.400 --> 00:07:02.440
[George] The six-day firestorm consumed the magnificent patriarchal cathedral of Santa Maria Mayor and the Royal Opera House of the Tagus, which had opened with lavish operatic performances barely seven months earlier.

44
00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:14.600
[George] Priceless ecclesiastical reliquaries, gold monstrances studded with Brazilian diamonds, and ancient illuminated Latin missiles dating back to the Reconquista were consumed by the white hot inferno.

45
00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:33.280
[George] The destruction of Lisbon sent a profound philosophical shockwave through European intellectual circles, shattering the popular optimistic philosophy of Gottfried Wilhelm Leibniz and Alexander Pope, who argued that whatever is right, and that this was the best of all possible worlds.

46
00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:47.120
[George] Philosophers asked, why would a benevolent God choose all saints' day to annihilate a deeply devout Catholic city, crushing pious worshippers inside churches while Lisbon's red light brothels on the hills were largely spared.

47
00:07:47.120 --> 00:08:04.640
[George] The French philosopher Voltaire was so shaken by the tragedy that he wrote his famous poem on the Lisbon disaster and his satirical masterpiece Candide, violently attacking philosophical optimism and calling for human empathy and practical action in the face of blind, indifferent nature.

48
00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:09.600
[George] Jean Jacques Rousseau entered the debate, arguing that nature was not to blame.

49
00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:18.480
[George] Human beings were responsible for cramming twenty thousand people into tall, unsafe stone tenements rather than living scattered in harmony with nature.

50
00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:31.520
[George] In Candide, Voltaire used the horrors of the Lisbon earthquake and the subsequent auto-daffé conducted by the Portuguese Inquisition to mock religious fatalism and emphasize the human duty to cultivate our own garden.

51
00:08:31.520 --> 00:08:46.320
[George] Voltaire's philosophical outrage over Lisbon challenged the dominant theological narrative that natural disasters were divine retribution, advocating instead for humanitarian compassion and scientific investigation of seismic phenomena.

52
00:08:46.320 --> 00:09:01.560
[George] The intellectual debate catalyzed by the Lisbon earthquake marked a decisive turning point in the history of ideas, accelerating the secularization of European thought and inspiring Immanuel Kant to publish three foundational treatises on physical geography.

53
00:09:01.800 --> 00:09:13.640
[George] Amidst the paralyzing despair, while King Joseph I cowered in tents in the royal gardens, suffering from permanent claustrophobia, one man stepped into the vacuum with iron determination.

54
00:09:13.640 --> 00:09:18.280
[George] The Secretary of State, Sebastian José de Carvalho e Milo.

55
00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:20.920
[George] Later created the Marx de Pombel.

56
00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:30.440
[George] When the trembling monarch asked what could be done in the face of divine wrath, Pombel uttered his famous, pragmatic command bury the dead and feed the living.

57
00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:36.840
[George] Pombal mobilized the army to prevent looting, erecting gallows in the ruins where dozens of looters were hanged.

58
00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:45.080
[George] He loaded corpses onto barges, took them out to the deep Atlantic, and buried them at sea to prevent deadly outbreaks of the plague.

59
00:09:45.080 --> 00:09:54.920
[George] He seized grain warehouses to distribute flour, fixed bread prices, and ordered provincial militias to wring the city to prevent merchants from abandoning the capital.

60
00:09:54.920 --> 00:09:58.600
[George] Within weeks, Pombol had brought order out of Apocalypse.

61
00:09:58.600 --> 00:10:10.840
[George] Pombol's gallows stood prominently on the hills surrounding Lisbon, where armed troops summarily hanged over thirty deserters and looters to maintain public safety and deter lawlessness in the devastated city.

62
00:10:10.840 --> 00:10:23.880
[George] Pombal's swift emergency measures prevented catastrophic post-disaster famine and epidemic disease, distributing over 50,000 loaves of bread daily from centralized relief bakeries erected in public parks.

63
00:10:23.880 --> 00:10:35.640
[George] Pombel lived in his carriage parked in the central square for three weeks without changing his clothes, holding emergency council meetings by lantern light while dictating decrees that restored civil order.

64
00:10:35.640 --> 00:10:52.320
[George] Rather than rebuilding Lisbon on its medieval, labyrinthine street pattern, Pombel engaged visionary military engineers, Manuel de Maya and Eugenio dos Santos, to raise the ruins and build a revolutionary modern capital, the Baksa Pombolina.

65
00:10:52.320 --> 00:11:03.280
[George] They engineered a neoclassical grid of wide, airy avenues flanked by standardized four-story stone buildings, designed specifically to withstand future seismic shocks.

66
00:11:03.280 --> 00:11:18.880
[George] Most revolutionary was the Gaiola Pombolina, the Pomboline Cage, a flexible internal wooden architectural framework that could sway during an earthquake without collapsing, tested by having battalions of soldiers march in rhythm around scale wooden models.

67
00:11:18.880 --> 00:11:28.560
[George] On the riverfront where the royal palace had stood, Pombal created the majestic Prasa do Cameacio, welcoming maritime trade from around the globe.

68
00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:41.040
[George] Lisbon rose from the ashes as the first earthquake-resistant, rationally planned neoclassical city in the Western world, a triumphant monument to enlightenment reason and human resilience in the face of catastrophe.

69
00:11:41.040 --> 00:11:54.800
[George] The Bakes's straight avenues were paved with distinctive black and white limestone mosaics known as Calzada Portuguessa, while the standardized buildings were painted in cheerful ochre and rose hues to banish the memories of disaster.

70
00:11:54.800 --> 00:12:08.240
[George] The Pomboline buildings of the Baksa remain an engineering marvel of 18th-century civil architecture, their symmetrical facades and earthquake-resistant timber cages protecting Lisbon through centuries of subsequent tremors.

71
00:12:08.240 --> 00:12:21.880
[George] The wide boulevards of the new Baiksa were designed with geometric symmetry and lined with uniform neoclassical facades, creating an enduring architectural testament to the triumph of human reason over natural chaos.

72
00:12:21.600 --> 00:12:25.560
[George] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

73
00:12:25.760 --> 00:12:33.320
[Rebecca] Next week, our European Odyssey travels to the sun drenched marble temples of the Aegean Sea with episode 234.

74
00:12:29.680 --> 00:12:40.120
